\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\title{Does a Structured Peer-Feedback Protocol Improve Online Course Persistence?}
\author{Anonymous}
\date{April 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Study Design}
We ran a semester-long intervention on a university online course. Students were assigned to a control section with the usual discussion forum or to a treatment section with a structured peer-feedback protocol. Table~\ref{tab:participants} summarizes enrollment and completion by cohort.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Enrolled participants by cohort.}
\label{tab:participants}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Cohort & Enrolled & Completed \\
\midrule
Autumn & 251 & 197 \\
Spring & 232 & 184 \\
\midrule
Total & 483 & 381 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Main Results}
Table~\ref{tab:main} reports the primary outcome, the weekly persistence score, by group for the Spring cohort, for which the manual audit of the enrollment records has just been completed.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Primary outcome by group (Spring cohort).}
\label{tab:main}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Group & $n$ & Mean & SD \\
\midrule
Control & 214 & 3.41 & 0.62 \\
Treatment & 209 & 3.86 & 0.58 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The treatment group shows a higher mean persistence score than the control group, and the gap is consistent with the pooled estimate reported in the pre-registration.

\section{Robustness}
Table~\ref{tab:robust} repeats the contrast within year-of-study subgroups to check whether the effect is carried by a single population.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Subgroup contrasts in persistence score (treatment minus control).}
\label{tab:robust}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Subgroup & Mean difference \\
\midrule
Lower years & 0.39 \\
Upper years & 0.52 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Discussion}
The pattern of results suggests that the protocol helps students survive the mid-semester slump rather than attracting already-persistent students at enrollment. We caution that the persistence score blends login frequency with submission timeliness, and future cohorts will separate the two components.
\end{document}
